{
  "version": 2,
  "name": "45 deg 2F Chamfer Bit - 3.175mm Shank",
  "shape": "chamfer.fcstd",
  "shape-type": "Chamfer",
  "parameter": {
    "Chipload": "0.0333 mm",
    "CuttingEdgeAngle": 90.0,
    "CuttingEdgeHeight": "1.5375 mm",
    "CuttingEdgeLength": "2.174 mm",
    "Diameter": "3.175 mm",
    "Flutes": 2,
    "Length": "38 mm",
    "Material": "Carbide",
    "ShankDiameter": "3.175 mm",
    "SpindleDirection": "Forward",
    "TipDiameter": "0.1 mm"
  },
  "attribute": {
    "SpindleSpeed": "12000",
    "HorizFeed": "800 mm/min",
    "VertFeed": "300 mm/min",
    "RampFeed": "400 mm/min",
    "Stepover": "1.0 mm",
    "Stepdown": "0.75 mm",
    "RampAngle": "2 deg",
    "SourceNote": "Makera 'Chamfering Bit - 1/8\" Shank', hardwood preset, re-derived for this repo from vendor/CarveraProfiles/.../Makera_Chamfering_Bit_-_1_8_Shank_Hardwood.fctb. CuttingEdgeAngle 90 deg is the INCLUDED angle -> 45 deg per side, which is the chamfer angle the flank cuts. Feeds pulled back from the vendor's 1000 mm/min to 800 (0.033 mm/tooth at 12000 RPM x 2F) in line with the ~2/3-of-preset rule the other Carvera Air ops on this machine settled on (docs/carvera-air.md, 'Audible Profile'). CuttingEdgeHeight CORRECTED 2026-08-15: the vendor file said 3 mm, which is geometrically impossible for a 45 deg bit of this diameter - the cone spans (Diameter - TipDiameter)/2 = 1.5375 mm of height (2.174 mm along the flank), and ABOVE the cone is the unchamfered 3.175 mm shank with no relief. Commanding the tip deeper than 1.5375 mm below the top face makes the shank plow a vertical wall into the workpiece (proven on an epoxy test block). Keep commanded width + tip clearance under 1.5375; see docs/chamfer-bits.md. NOTE the 38 mm overall length: this bit sits far shallower in the spindle than the 80 mm 6 mm router, so its tip reaches HIGHER (a shorter stickout = a higher tip at the same nose height) - which is the only reason the top edge of a 70 mm hardwood brick is reachable at all."
  }
}
